.TH STTY 1 2026-10-22 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
stty \- print or change console input settings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stty
.RB [ \-a ]
.br
.B stty
.IR setting ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B stty
prints or changes the input mode of the console attached to standard
input, and prints the size of the console window.
On Unix it controls a terminal device.
Windows consoles have no such device, so this build recognizes only the
settings listed below, and every other Unix setting is an invalid
argument.
.PP
Standard input must be a console.
When it is a file or a pipe,
.B stty
prints an error and exits with status 1, even when the only argument is
.BR size .
.PP
With no arguments,
.B stty
prints the settings that differ from those set by
.BR sane .
The only setting it can report is echo, so it prints
.B \-echo
when echo is off and nothing at all when echo is on.
With
.BR \-a ,
it prints the window size and the echo setting on two lines:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
rows 30; columns 120;
echo
.EE
.RE
.PP
Neither form reports whether the console is in raw mode.
.PP
With one or more settings,
.B stty
first checks every argument, and changes nothing if any is invalid.
It then applies the settings in order, so a later setting overrides an
earlier one, and writes the resulting mode to the console once.
.B size
is the only setting that prints anything.
.SS Settings
These change the console's input mode, a set of flags that Windows
keeps for the console.
The change stays in effect after
.B stty
exits, for every program that reads from the same console, until a
program changes the mode again.
Flags that no setting names, such as quick edit mode and virtual
terminal input, are left as they are.
.TP
.B echo
Display typed characters as the console reads a line.
.TP
.B \-echo
Read lines without displaying them.
.TP
.BR cooked ", " \-raw
Turn on line input and processed input.
With line input, a read returns only when Enter is pressed, and the
console provides line editing.
With processed input, Ctrl+C interrupts the program instead of being
read as a character.
.TP
.BR raw ", " \-cooked
Turn off line input and processed input, so that a read returns each
key as it is typed and Ctrl+C is read as an ordinary character.
Echo is not changed.
Windows allows echo only with line input, so use
.B raw
together with
.BR \-echo .
.TP
.B sane
Turn on echo, line input, and processed input.
.TP
.B size
Print the height and then the width of the console window, in character
cells, as two decimal numbers separated by a space.
This is the size of the visible window, not of the screen buffer behind
it.
It does not change the console.
.PP
The size comes from the console attached to standard output or, when
that is not a console, to standard error.
The console on standard input is not asked, though standard input must
still be a console.
When neither standard output nor standard error is a console,
.B size
prints an error message instead of the size, and
.B \-a
leaves out the size line.
The variables
.B LINES
and
.B COLUMNS
override the size; see
.BR ENVIRONMENT .
.PP
The settings
.BR rows ,
.BR cols ,
and
.BR columns ,
which resize the window on Unix, are recognized but always fail:
a number after one of them is reported as an invalid argument.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-a
Print the window size and the echo setting.
.B \-a
cannot be combined with a setting.
Letters after the dash may repeat, as in
.BR \-aa ,
but any letter other than
.B a
makes the argument invalid.
.TP
.B \-\-help
When it is the first argument, print a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0.
In any other position it is an invalid argument.
.PP
There are no
.B \-F
and
.B \-g
options, and
.B \-\-
is an invalid argument.
An argument that is a single
.B \-
is ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LINES
When set, replaces the height reported by
.B size
and
.BR \-a ,
even when a console is present.
.TP
.B COLUMNS
When set, replaces the width in the same way.
.PP
A value that is not a number from 2 to 29999 counts as 24 for
.B LINES
and 80 for
.BR COLUMNS .
When either variable is set and there is no console to ask, the other
dimension is reported as 24 or 80 rather than as an error.
.PP
The startup code shared by all applets reads
.BR BB_CRITICAL_ERROR_DIALOGS ,
.BR BB_GLOBBING ,
and
.BR BB_UMASK ;
see
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
This includes a
.B size
or
.B \-a
that could not find the window size.
.TP
.B 1
An argument was invalid,
.B \-a
was combined with a setting, standard input is not a console, or the
console rejected the new mode.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the height and width of the console:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ stty size
.EE
.RE
.PP
Save the height and width in shell variables:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ set \-\- $(stty size)
$ rows=$1 cols=$2
.EE
.RE
.PP
Restore the usual input mode after a program left the console in raw
mode:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ stty sane
.EE
.RE
.PP
Turn off line input and echo, and later turn them back on:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ stty raw \-echo
$ stty sane
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
The settings affect programs that read the console with ordinary reads.
The kit's shell reads keys directly, both for its command line and for
its
.B read
builtin, so it echoes and edits input whatever the console mode is.
In particular,
.B stty \-echo
does not hide input to
.BR read ;
use
.B read \-s
instead.
.PP
There is no way to save the current mode and restore it later, because
.B \-g
is not supported.
.PP
When
.BR rows ,
.BR cols ,
or
.B columns
is the last argument, with no number after it,
.B stty
crashes instead of reporting an error.
.PP
.BR ttysize (1)
also prints the console size, prints the width first by default, and
does not require standard input to be a console.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR reset (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR ttysize (1)
